// Musical: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== musical.f01 | feature | three-act
title: The Singing Strike
genre: musical + historical-drama
tone: Freezing, many-voiced and lifted
era: 1912 | period
setting: The textile mills of a Massachusetts river town | fictional | town
logline: When a mill town's wages are cut by the price of three loaves, twenty thousand workers who share no common language walk out in January, and discover that the only thing they can all do together is sing.
premise: The mills employ people from twenty-five countries who do not eat, pray or speak together, which is how the owners like it. A new law shortens the week by two hours; the mills shorten the pay to match. {anna}, a Polish weaver, is the first to stop her loom. On the picket line nobody can understand anybody. Somebody starts a song from home.
synopsis: The first number is the mill itself, looms as percussion, and a pay envelope opened. {anna} shuts down her frame and shouts "short pay" in a language half the room does not have; the gesture is understood. By night the strike is twenty thousand strong and a Babel. The organiser sent from New York, {joe}, speaks English and Italian and is out of his depth by the first afternoon. The second act is the invention of a method. {carmela}, a Sicilian mender with a church-choir voice, and {anna} cannot speak to each other but can sing in thirds. The committee runs meetings in translation relays, and marches in a moving line the militia cannot disperse because it never stops, singing in turn: Polish hymns, Italian opera, Syrian laments, a Yiddish lullaby, a French round. The mill agent, {wood_agent}, brings in soldiers with bayonets; there is a death on the line, and the leaders are jailed for a murder a policeman committed. The children are hungry. The idea that turns the strike is domestic: send the children away, to families in other cities who will feed them, and let the country see them get off the train. The song for that is the quietest in the film. At the station the police club mothers on the platform in front of the newspapers. Congress holds hearings; a girl of fourteen, {teresa}, testifies about the machine that took her scalp. In the third act the owners settle: a raise for the lowest paid first. The final number is the one the strike left behind without knowing it, put together on screen out of a line on a picket sign, one voice at a time in every language: bread, and roses too. {anna} and {carmela} still cannot hold a conversation. They go back in through the gate arm in arm.
question: What do people who cannot talk to each other have in common?
themes: solidarity across language; dignity as well as wages; women as organisers
audience: General; families with older children
viewpoint: Ensemble, anchored by {anna} and {carmela}
stakes: Families at starvation's edge, the leaders' lives, and whether an unorganisable workforce can win
conflict: Twenty-five nationalities and one winter against owners who have always relied on their being strangers
ending: The strike is won. The two women return to the looms as friends without a shared sentence.
experience: Goosebumps from massed voices, anger at a railway platform, and a finale that sends you out humming
look: Snow and brick, breath in the air, a river of dark coats; song numbers lit by the same grey daylight, never by fantasy
sound: Looms as rhythm; a dozen musical traditions braided into one march
m: win | institution | self-vs-others | triumph
@ anna | Anna Lopata | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 29 | Thirty-two cents back in the envelope | It is three loaves | She can speak to one worker in twenty | Hot-tempered, funny, fearless | From stopping one loom to leading a line that never stops | Polish, with ten words of English used like tools | Shawl, weaver's hook at her belt
@ carmela | Carmela Russo | co-lead | ally | human | she/her | 33 | Her daughter safe and fed | The machines have already hurt her child | She has never defied anyone in her life | Devout, shy, with a voice that fills a street | From the back of the hall to the front of the march | Sicilian; sings what she cannot say | Black dress, a medal of a saint
@ joe | Joe Santoro | supporting | mentor | human | he/him | 26 | To win one, properly | He has seen strikes broken by division | He is jailed halfway through | Eloquent, organised, humble enough to learn from them | From leading to being led by what they invent | Fast New York English and Italian | Soft hat, a notebook
@ wood_agent | Mr. Harlan Whitcombe | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 55 | The mills running at the old cost | His bonus is the margin | The whole country is watching a railway platform | Civil, cold, baffled by singing | From contempt to a signature | Boston vowels | Fur collar, a watch chain
@ teresa | Teresa Russo | supporting | none | human | she/her | 14 | To say what happened to her | A machine took her hair and scalp | She must say it to senators | Solemn, exact, brave | From silent to heard by a nation | Small, clear, bilingual | Cap pulled low, good shoes borrowed
~ anna - carmela | weaver and mender | no shared language | a harmony, and an arm through an arm
~ anna - joe | striker and organiser | he explains strikes to her | she explains this one to him
~ carmela - teresa | mother and daughter | protection | pride in a hearing room
# Short Pay | INT | Weave Room | DAY | anna, carmela | Stop the looms | Looms as rhythm. Envelopes. {anna} throws her shuttle and shouts. Frame after frame goes still until the room is silent and then is not | The walkout begins | 6 | crowd=300, dance, props=looms+pay envelopes
# Short Pay | EXT | Mill Gate, Night | NIGHT | anna, carmela, joe | Find a common tongue | A picket line in nine languages, failing. {carmela}, alone, starts a hymn. {anna} finds the third above it. The line turns to listen | Song becomes the method | 6 | crowd=400, vfx=snow
# The Moving Line | EXT | Canal Street | DAY | anna, carmela, joe, wood_agent | Never stop | A chain of thousands walking round the mills, singing in turn, never standing still to be dispersed. Bayonets. A shot. A woman down | A striker is killed | 7 | crowd=800, dance, stunts=militia charge
# The Moving Line | INT | Strike Kitchen | NIGHT | carmela, teresa, anna | Decide about the children | Soup for two hundred. The idea: send them to other cities. The quietest song in the film, a lullaby in four languages | | 5 | crowd=100, props=soup kettles
# The Children | EXT | Railway Station, Platform | DAY | carmela, teresa, anna | Be seen | Mothers, children with name tags, a train. Police clubs, in front of reporters. {teresa} is pulled from her mother's hand | The country sees | 7 | crowd=300, stunts=police assault, vehicles=steam train
# The Children | INT | Congressional Hearing Room | DAY | teresa, wood_agent | Testify | A small girl takes off her cap for the senators. No song. {wood_agent} studies the table | | 4 | crowd=80
# Bread And Roses | EXT | Mill Gate, Morning | DAY | anna, carmela, joe, teresa, wood_agent | Go back in | Settlement read out, translated down the line like a wave. One voice, then another language, then all: bread, and roses too. Two women through the gate arm in arm | The strike is won | 7 | crowd=800, dance
review: authored | 2026-11-12 | written whole; drawn from the 1912 textile strike, with invented people and an invented town
